        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="container">
    <h1>ERP Integration Requirements Specification</h1>

    <div class="metadata">
        <p><strong>Document Type:</strong> Technical Requirements Specification (Draft)</p>
        <p><strong>Sender / Procurement:</strong> Majed Al-Ghamdi (Procurement Manager)</p>
        <p><strong>Project Stakeholders:</strong> Lina Bakr (Head of Sales), Abdulaziz Al-Mutairi (Finance Manager)</p>
        <p><strong>Target CRM Vendor:</strong> Vendor A (CloudCore)</p>
        <p><strong>Scope:</strong> CRM and ERP Integration Points, Data Flows, and Security Standards</p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Introduction and Objectives</h2>
    <p>This technical specification outlines the required integration architecture between the newly selected CRM platform (Vendor A - CloudCore) and the corporate Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) system. The integration ensures seamless data synchronization for the 40-person sales team, maintaining financial accuracy, operational efficiency, and strict adherence to regional compliance policies.</p>

    <h2>2. Integration Endpoints and Data Mapping</h2>
    <p>To facilitate bi-directional communication between the CRM and ERP systems, the following primary integration endpoints and data mapping rules must be established:</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Integration Point</th>
                <th>Direction</th>
                <th>Trigger Event</th>
                <th>Data Mapping Requirements</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Customer / Account Master</strong></td>
                <td>Bi-directional</td>
                <td>Account creation or update in CRM / ERP</td>
                <td>Company Name, Tax ID, Billing Address, Shipping Address, Credit Limit, Payment Terms.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Product Catalog & Pricing</strong></td>
                <td>ERP to CRM</td>
                <td>Catalog update in ERP</td>
                <td>SKU ID, Item Description, Unit Price, Tax Category, Inventory Availability Levels.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Sales Quotations</strong></td>
                <td>CRM to ERP</td>
                <td>Quote status changed to "Won / Approved"</td>
                <td>Quote ID, Customer ID, Line Items, Quantities, Agreed Pricing, Discounts, Sales Rep ID.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Sales Orders & Invoicing</strong></td>
                <td>Bi-directional</td>
                <td>Order conversion / Invoice generation</td>
                <td>Order ID, ERP Invoice ID, Payment Status, Delivery Status, Aging Details.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>3. Data Flows and Synchronization Architecture</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Synchronization Frequency:</strong> Master data (Products, Accounts) will sync on a scheduled batch basis (hourly), while transactional data (Quotations, Orders) will utilize real-time Webhooks/API calls to ensure immediate processing.</li>
        <li><strong>Error Handling & Logging:</strong> Failed API transactions must be logged in an integration middleware queue with automated email alerts dispatched to the IT and Finance teams (Abdulaziz Al-Mutairi).</li>
        <li><strong>Language and Localization Support:</strong> Data flows must fully support UTF-8 encoding to accurately transmit and store Arabic character sets across both CRM and ERP environments.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Security and Authentication Requirements</h2>
    <p>Given the sensitivity of financial and customer data, the integration architecture must enforce rigorous security standards:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Authentication Protocol:</strong> All API requests between the CRM and ERP must use OAuth 2.0 token-based authentication with restricted scopes and automated token rotation.</li>
        <li><strong>Data Encryption:</strong> All data in transit must be encrypted using TLS 1.3. Data at rest within integration staging databases must be encrypted using AES-256.</li>
        <li><strong>Data Residency Compliance:</strong> In alignment with regional regulatory requirements, all integration endpoints, middleware logs, and data sync caches must reside strictly within data centers located in Saudi Arabia (KSA).</li>
        <li><strong>Access Control:</strong> Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) must be implemented to restrict API integration permissions strictly to designated service accounts.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note">
        <strong>Status Notice:</strong> This document represents a draft specification prepared by Procurement (Majed Al-Ghamdi) for review by Sales and Finance stakeholders. Final architectural implementation and vendor deployment depend on formal technical validation provided by the project team.
    </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

